hey guys Greg knuckles here with string  theory calm back with the fourth  installment of our semi daily Q&amp;A and  the question today is if specificity is  very important for building strength for  mastering the motor skills of the  specific movements that you're using to  test strength why use exercise  variations in the first place so in the  context of powerlifting if you're trying  to get your squat bench deadlift up why  would you ever do anything other than  just the competition variants of squat  bench and deadlift you know why would  you do front squats close grip bench  deadlift with the opposite stance block  poles etc and it comes down to two basic  reasons the first has to do with motor  learning there's a theory in just  learning in general that a lot of how we  learn isn't just from repetition but is  from error identification and correction  and so as you practice a movement you  get really good with it and it's easy to  kind of go on autopilot it's it's easy  to to tune out a little bit as you're  doing it so you're you're a little bit  less engaged or just you get really  really strong and the weights for the  competition variant become very very  challenging and you're more just kind of  focused on getting the weight up than  the actual execution of the lift and  mastering it and so when you introduce  exercise variations there's their  movements that you're a little bit less  familiar with so errors and execution  are going to be much much more obvious  much much more noticeable so you get  more opportunities for identifying  errors and correcting them with very  similar movements that are then going to  transfer to the main lifts so say in the  case of the squat you know if you're a  low bar squatter in competition front  squat a high bar  squat Paula squat those are all still  training the general motor skill of the  squat but by introducing those  variations you're going to kind of throw  yourself into waters that are a little  bit less comfortable so you're going to  make more errors be able to identify  more errors and master that general  motor skill of squatting that you'll be  able to then transfer over to your low  bar squating competition you you see  this in a lot of motor learning research  in general as well if you want you can  just run a quick search for constant  versus varied to practice in almost any  motor skill the vast vast majority of  the time varied practice beats out  constant practice and and this is kind  of the theory behind it that if you're  doing the exact same thing all the time  you just don't have as many  opportunities to identify and correct  errors now if you're training with a  coach I think it is a lot easier to  maybe make your training a little bit  more vanilla and that be very successful  you see this with say borscht Eiko's  lifters they pretty much only stick to  squat and bench they do have some more  variation in their deadlift training but  if you have a coach watching you and you  know pointing out things that you can  improve with each rep even if you do  kind of have that natural inclination to  just sort of go on autopilot with  movements that you've done a lot that  you're very very comfortable with under  a coach's watchful eye that's going to  help with error identification and  correction you know giving you things to  watch out for on the next set next  workout etc so I don't I certainly don't  think that exercise variation is  necessary but I do think that that is  one of the biggest benefits for most  people including it in their program  it's just going to you know give you  something you're not quite as accustomed  to to help you better master the general  motor skills of squatting benching dead  lifting so that's number two or that's  number one number two is that just by  including some slight variation that's  probably going to help  reduce your risk of overuse injuries now  I'm not going to pretend like there's  research to support this but again in  general if you look at pretty much any  other sport in existence the the more  you're doing the exact same thing over  and over and over the higher your risk  of overuse injuries is so for example  just by analogy  runners tend to have a much much higher  injury risk than triathletes do because  all they're doing for the most part is  running and so a lot of running coaches  recommend cross-training to their  runners when they're not in competitive  season just to cut down on that risk of  overuse injury whereas you know  triathletes they're biking swimming  running all the time so they're they do  have a little bit more variety there so  they tend to have lower rates of overuse  injury just for myself and in people  I've coached in just observing athletes  in the gym I do I do tend to find that  people who do have a little bit more  variety in their training their their  results may not be quite as good in the  short term because they aren't getting  that highly highly specific practice and  that that would especially be true for  may be newer lifters or intermediate  lifters but I do find that they end up  not having to take as much time off with  overuse injuries whereas people who use  really high volumes it's just a very  small array of exercises a lot of them  do tend to struggle more with overuse  injuries that's just an observation I've  made so to answer this question summing  up the video again two biggest benefits  of including some more variation is one  the general motor learning benefits of  giving you more opportunities more  abilities to identify and correct errors  and technique that may not be quite as  obvious if you're just sticking with a  smaller set of movements and the other  is that I do think it's going to be  beneficial for helping you reduce your  risk of overuse injuries so you can stay  in the gym and keep training hard okay  so that is the video if you liked it  please like and subscribe and if you  have questions you want answered ask  them in the comments below and if I like  your question I will pick it and answer  it in our video tomorrow alright guys  until next time
 

